/reviews/wi-860-review-threat-model/001-ADJUDICATE-784e047.md`) returned SR-233, LLR-311 and TC-332 with one drafted follow-up. Under the owner's ruling of 2026-10-06 (a return is answered in the lane, not minted), it is answered here:\n \n-```toml\n-title = \"Re-close SR-233's chain: an honest parent link, every finding-judging brief, a named dismissal record, and a TC that pins the definition\"\n-workstream = \"process\"\n-buildtier = \"medium\"\n-sr_refs = [\"SR-233\"]\n-priority = 8\n-```\n-\n-SR-233, LLR-311 and TC-332 were returned on first approval. The built PROCESS.md §6 paragraph and the reviewer brief's pointer are not in question. Scope of this follow-up: re-text the three rows and strengthen the one test, then bring all three back for first approval.\n+- SR-233 drops the coincident claim SN-006 does not carry, and its acceptance criteria name the ruling party and the lane's delegated-decisions record and bind every delivered brief that judges a review finding, not only the reviewer brief.\n+- LLR-311 names the home by its real form, the §6 bold-lead paragraph \"Review threat model\", and follows SR-233's widened scope.\n+- The pinning test asserts the definition's content (the in-scope classes, the exclusion, the one-recorded-line dismissal, \"never answered with code\") and the absence of all four boundary examples from every shipped prompt (`5c170072`); TC-332's Method, Expected and Evidence state exactly that.\n \n-1. SR-233 `Coincident`: remove the claim that SN-006 requires a run to avoid \"unbounded effort on conditions it cannot remedy\". SN-006 carries no such clause, and the row's own Rationale correctly labels it DERIVED. Either drop the coincident waiver or replace it with a true statement of how the row delivers its need. If the bound is only delivered once a dispute is ruled, name the sibling that rules it (WI-865's row) as a joint deliverer rather than claiming coincidence.\n-2. SR-233 AcceptanceCriteria: widen \"the delivered reviewer bri