{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfqhLvi459Rm9QaEFPgC6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I returned all three rows again, so the outcome is `OUTCOME: RETURN rows=3`. The verdict is committed as `7d229521` with the `WI: WI-860` trailer, and the pre-commit hook passed. Because I approved nothing, no registry cell changed and I ran no snapshot.\n\n**What the rework fixed:** three of the four points from the first sitting.\n- The false `Coincident` cell is gone.\n- LLR-311 now names the definition's home correctly, as the §6 bold-lead paragraph.\n- TC-332 and its test now check the paragraph's content and all four boundary examples, not just the link.\n\n**What's still wrong is the new clause about where a dismissal is recorded.** SR-233's acceptance criteria and LLR-311's Detail say the ruling party records each dismissal in \"the lane's delegated-decisions record\".\n- **The record often doesn't exist.** PROCESS_OPTIONS.md says that record applies only when a run acts on the owner's behalf, and its setting ships `\"off\"`. So in an attended review, or for an adopter on the default, the obligation can't be met. Yet SR-233 binds every review.\n- **The built text doesn't say it.** The §6 paragraph still says only \"dismissed in one recorded line\", with no ruling party and no record. LLR-311 describes text the module doesn't contain.\n- **Two clauses are unchecked.** TC-332 checks neither the recording clause nor the clause that every brief judging a review finding must cite the definition. It only checks the reviewer brief.\n\n**The follow-up:** I drafted one item in WI-860's spec `## Dispositions`, and the intake parser reads it cleanly. It asks the lane to:\n- limit the recording clause to runs where that record applies and give every other review a home that always exists;\n- add that wording to the §6 paragraph itself;\n- have TC-332 check the recording clause, and either check each brief that